A||||0||FieroLT1||Rodney Dickman's Rack bushing installation...||||1||
Z||000000||FieroLT1||03-19-2001||11:41 AM||||This weekend I gave to the chore of doing ome needed front end work. The goal was to try to get rid of a right wheel slop I had. I wound up replacing the wheel bearing, a lower ball joint, the outer tie rod and changed to polyurethane grease boots. But the biggest source of the slop was coming from the steering rack. I pealed off the steering rack boot and the inner tie rod look (felt) pretty solid. The steering rack bushing, however, was not. I could jiggle it about 1/8" up and down, back and forth. Since I was going to go through the trouble of replacing this, I thought I might as well upgrade from the wimpy GM plastic to more durable aluminum. So, I ordered Rodney Dickman's all aluminum steering rack bushing. This piece is extremely well machined to very tight tolerances. Very nice indeed. I had a bit of a problem following the included instructions, so I came up with my own way. Not that this is the best way to do it, but is what worked for me.<BR>The main goal was to install the bushing WITHOUT dropping the rack. I you drop or remove the rack this will be much, much easier. First the inner tie rod has to be removed. A big wrench with a fair amount of force will break it loose. Second, and to my surprise, the bushing did not slide right in. I guess there must've been some kind of distortion on the edge of the steering rack shaft that prevented it going in. I had to tap it with a 30mm socket. After that outer edge clears the inner part of the bushing, then it slides right in by hand and sits perfectly inside the rack. Then it comes the hard part. The drilling of the holes. Instructions called for two holes to be drilled and then riveted to hold the bushing in place. Well, first thing was, there is no way to get a drill in there. The edge of the rack sits right on top of the frame rail (on my 88 anyway). So, with the help of one of those 90 degree angle drill adapters, I managed to drill one hole, pass the rack boot. A second hole was next to impossible to drill. Trying to make the best out of this one hole. I bypassed Rodney's included rivets, and decided to tap that hole and installed a hex set screw. This, along with some locktite, made this a very sound installation. Believe me, that bushing is going no where.  The end result was like night and day. That rack is unbelievable solid now. No slop whatsoever. I highly recommend this upgrade if you have even a little slop or shake there. The steering now is 90% more responsive. And the feedback from the road has increased twice fold. I am very happy with Rodney's steering rack aluminum bushing. I wish I had it done sooner.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>regards,<P>------------------<BR>Winston Ojeda<BR><B>88 Formula LT1</B><BR>Milwaukee, Wisconsin, USA<BR><A HREF="http://www.FieroLT1.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.FieroLT1.com</A><BR><I>Home of the Fiero LT1</I><BR> <A HREF="mailto:wojeda@FieroLT1.com">wojeda@FieroLT1.com</A> ||||reg||1
